#{[17592186093403 :tutorial/content "We know that we can write a system of three linear equations in three variables in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n- $A$ is the $3\\times 3$ coefficient matrix,\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}$ is the column vector of variables, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{b}$ is the column vector of constants.\n\nIf the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ by \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {I}_{3}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nLet's see how this works for a particular system in the next example."] [17592186100695 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{3}} & 4 & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & -2 & {\\color{lightgray}{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $3$rd row in $A$ by the $2$nd column in $B$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107082 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=2 \\\\ 2x-y-z &=-1 \\\\ 2y-6z &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad M = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=2 \\\\ 0x+y-3z &=-5 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=2 \\\\ y-3z &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe **basic variables** are the variables of our system whose coefficients are the pivots of the row echelon form matrix. Let's illustrate the pivots of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nThe pivots are\n\n- $m_{11}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $x,$ and\n\n- $m_{22}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $y.$\n\nTherefore, the basic variables are $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe remaining variables are called the **free variables**. We have only one free variable in our system, which is $z$.\n\nFinally, we can describe the **general solution** by finding equations for the basic variables in terms of the free variables. In our case, this means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z$.\n\nTo do this, we apply the back substitution. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny - 3z = -5\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = -5+3z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-5+3z)+z=2\\,⟹\\, & x=-3+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3+2z \\\\ y &=-5+3z \\\\ z &=z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}= \\begin{bmatrix}-3+2z \\\\ -5+3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\n\n\nGeometrically, this is the vector equation of a line, and any point on the line is a solution to our original system.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $z$ and find the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the line. For example, if we choose $z=0,$ then the corresponding values are $x=-3$ and $y=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-3,$ $y=-5,$ $z=0.$"] [17592186121022 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we will consider a few more properties of determinants. Let's start with the following important property. \n\n*The determinant of the product of two square matrices is equal to the product of the determinants.*\n\nSymbolically, if $M$ and $N$ are both square matrices, then\n\n$$\n\\det  ( M \\cdot  N ) = \\det  ( M ) \\cdot  \\det  ( N ) .\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to find the determinant of the matrix $A$ that is defined as follows:\n$$\nA = \n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - 2 & - 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}3 & - 10 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo easily compute $\\det(A)$, we first compute the determinants of the two matrices and then multiply the results. Also, notice that both matrices in our product are triangular, so their determinants are given by the product of the diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -10 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ -2 & {\\color{red}{-3}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 2 & 0 & {\\color{red}{2}}\\end{vmatrix}}_{\\text{lower triangular}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{3}} & -10 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{5}} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}_{\\text{upper triangular}} \\\\  & =({\\color{red}{1}}\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-3}})\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}})\\cdot ({\\color{red}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{5}}\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-1}})) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot (-15) \\\\  & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186121046 :tutorial/content "One way to simplify a determinant calculation is to create a row (or column) containing all zeros except for one entry. Then, we can use a Laplace expansion across that row (or column) to compute the determinant.\n\nLet's demonstrate this method on the matrix $A$ below.\n\n$$\nA=\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}2 & 1 & {\\color{black}1} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {\\color{black}3} \\\\ 8 & 14 & 17\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can make entries to the right of $a_{21}=1$ equal to zero using elementary *column* operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one column to another column in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.167em}\\phantom{=}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & 1 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}} & 14 & 17\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\hspace{2em}\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}: &={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & -3 & -5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{red}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}} & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.167em}\\phantom{=}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & -3 & -5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{red}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}} & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{1}}\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(21-10) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186121057 :tutorial/content "We can build some geometric intuition for finite-dimensional vector spaces. To illustrate, let's find the dimensions of $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\},$ $\\mathbb{R},$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nFirst, note that $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^1.$ We have the following standard bases:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^0:\n\t\\big\\{\n\t\t\\textrm{no basis}\n\t\\big\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^1:\n\t\\big\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}\n\t\\big\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^2:\n\t\\Bigg\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{j}}\n\t\\Bigg\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^3:\n\t\\Bigg\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{j}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{k}}\n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have the following dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}({\\mathbb{R}}^{0}) &=0,\\hspace{1em}\\text{dim}({\\mathbb{R}}^{1})=1,\\hspace{1em}\\text{dim}({\\mathbb{R}}^{2})=2,\\hspace{1em}\\text{dim}({\\mathbb{R}}^{3})=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's build some geometric intuition. We can represent the standard bases in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/16/169b5597d89fa32fd09772593cc2a9a330ee12881281dea4e3e43dc6165b9ebd.png)\n\nSo, the sequence $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ $\\mathbb{R}^1,$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ $\\ldots$ can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/37/379823eca26d43337744a8f4a3872e67ac537a6c193d55e39b5d6761f258f6f5.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^0$ is represented geometrically as a $0$-dimensional point.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^1$ is represented geometrically as a $1$-dimensional line segment.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is represented geometrically as a $2$-dimensional square.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is represented geometrically as a $3$-dimensional cube.\n\nUnfortunately, we can't visualize dimensions greater than $3,$ but we know that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ has the following standard basis consisting of $n$ vectors:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\cdots, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n) = n.\n$$"] [17592186265828 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{3}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 3 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{3}} & -2 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{10}} & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $1$st row in $U$ by the $3$rd column in $V$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{13} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-6-6+5 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265831 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-7}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & -3 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{3}} & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $2$nd row in $A$ by the $4$th column in $B$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{24} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-9+4+3 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292857 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+2z &=5 \\\\ 2x+6y+5z &=22 \\\\ x-2y-3z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$1$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$6$</td><td>$5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-2$</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$x$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$y$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$z$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">.</span>\n</div>"] [17592186292866 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-3}} \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{1}} & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $2$nd row in $M$ by the $2$nd column in $N$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{l}_{22} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =8+6 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292893 :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+z &=4x+y+2 \\\\ x+y &=2z+2 \\\\ y &=x-y+z+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $x=[x,y,z]^{T}$ of the system by computing the inverse of the coefficient matrix.\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$x=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [17592186299537 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-y+z &=0 \\\\ 5x+y-7z &=-2 \\\\ -2x+4y+3z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-1$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$1$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-2$</td><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$x$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$y$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$z$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$4$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">.</span>\n</div>"] [17592186299540 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=11 \\\\ -x-3y-z &=0 \\\\ 8x+6y+3z &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>$6$</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$x$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$y$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$z$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-9$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">.</span>\n</div>"] [17592186299541 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+z &=1 \\\\ 3x-y+4z &=8 \\\\ -4x+y+5z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$1$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$1$</td><td>$5$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$x$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$y$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$z$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$8$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">.</span>\n</div>"] [17592186299542 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y+4z &=6 \\\\ y-9z &=-2 \\\\ x-y-8z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-2$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$x$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$y$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$z$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$6$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">.</span>\n</div>"] [17592186299685 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ -3 & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} \\\\ 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $3$rd row in $Q$ by the $1$st column in $R$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{31} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2(5)+1(-3)+(-2)(1) \\\\  & =10-3-2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299686 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 5 & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & -3 & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $1$st row in $Y$ by the $2$nd column in $Z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)(5)+3(-3)+0(1) \\\\  & =-10-9+0 \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299687 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nZ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & -3 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-4}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $2$nd row in $X$ by the $4$th column in $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{24} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-3)+2(2) \\\\  & =-9+4 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338852 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} \\\\ -4 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{6}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & -5 & {\\color{lightgray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -2 & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $2$nd row in $E$ by the $3$rd column in $F$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{23} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =20-6 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338853 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-3}} & {\\color{lightgray}{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-3}} & -2 & {\\color{lightgray}{-5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $1$st row in $M$ by the $2$nd column in $N$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}_{12} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-5+8 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338855 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{10}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-3}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} \\\\ -5 & 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & {\\color{lightgray}{-3}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} \\\\ -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{10}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} \\\\ -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{20}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $4$th row of $U$ by the $1$st column in $V$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{41} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot (-1)+10\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =10-3-10 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338856 :question/worked-solution "The full matrix product is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} \\\\ 3 & -4 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-6}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-8}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{3}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-9}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & 10 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the $2$nd row of $P$ by the $5$th column of $Q$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{a